# hilavitkutin-sym, Design Overview

`#![no_std]`, no alloc. Depends on `arvo` for the `bitfield!` macro and
`Bool`, `arvo-bits` for `Bits<N>`, and `notko` for `Maybe`. Generic
interned-identity core for the hilavitkutin ecosystem.

An interned identity is a small handle that stands in for a value, cheap
to copy and compared by integer equality. The string interner in
`hilavitkutin-str` is one instance of the idea: a string is folded to a
4-byte handle, equal strings yield equal handles, and the handle resolves
back to the string. That handle, its integer-equality identity, and the
two ways a value acquires one are not about strings. They generalise to
any domain that wants stable cheap identities, so they live here and the
string concern sits above as one consumer.

A domain acquires a handle one of two ways. It deduplicates an existing
value into a stable handle, which is interning: equal values give equal
handles and the handle resolves back to the value. Or it mints a fresh
unique handle with no backing value, which is generation: each mint is
distinct from every prior one and there is nothing to resolve. Strings
intern. A compiler pass that needs a fresh identity for a bound variable
it introduces generates. The core serves both from one handle type,
distinguishing the modes at the type level so a producer cannot be built
for the wrong one.

The handle carries a domain tag so that identities from different domains
are never confused. Two handles with different tags are never equal
whatever their ids, because the tag is part of the compared bits. That
disjointness by tag is the property consumers rely on: a minted identity
provably never equals an interned-string identity, not by luck of the
ids, but because the two live in different domains of one core.

## `Sym`, the identity handle

```rust
#[repr(transparent)]
#[derive(Copy, Clone, Eq, PartialEq, Hash, Debug, Default)]
pub struct Sym<S: SymShape = Standard>(S::Layout);
```

A 4-byte handle, integer equality across the whole 32-bit layout, `Copy`,
`repr(transparent)`, ABI-stable.

The equality, ordering and hashing impls are bound on the **layout**, not on the
shape. A derive would bound them on `S`, and `SymShape` requires only
`Copy + 'static`, so a legal shape that derives less would yield a handle with no
equality at all: the central promise absent by construction, in a way every
shipped shape hides by deriving everything.

How the 32 bits divide is a **shape**, not a constant of this crate: see `SymShape` below. The default shape, `Standard`, is
what every current consumer uses and what `SymLayout` declares via
`arvo_bits::bitfield!`:

| Bits | Field | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| 0 to 27 | `id` (28-bit) | 268M identities per domain, of which the low bits are a counter and the high bits an origin when the shape has more than one minter |
| 28 to 30 | `kind` (3-bit) | the domain tag, eight domains |
| 31 | `flag` (1-bit) | a domain-private flag; the string domain uses it for the const-versus-runtime origin, a domain that does not need it leaves it zero |

**A shape is a wire format.** Two `Sym`s built under different shapes divide
the same 32 bits differently, so they are not comparable and must not meet in
one store. A consumer choosing a shape other than `Standard` is choosing not to
interoperate with handles baked under `Standard`.

`SymLayout` generates per-field accessors (`kind()`, `id()`, `flag()`),
setters, and per-field mask constants. `Sym` exposes `Sym::new(kind, id)`
(flag zero) and `Sym::with_flag(self, bit)` to build a handle, plus
`kind()`, `id()`, `flag()`, and `to_bits()` (the raw `Bits<32, Hot>` view
for tests and persistence). Two `Sym`s with different `kind` values are
never equal, whatever their ids or flags, because `kind` is part of the
compared layout, and that is what keeps domains disjoint.

## `SymShape`, how the 32 bits divide

```rust
pub trait SymShape {
    // widths, and the Bits types at those widths
}
```

The layout policy, lifted out of the substrate. Three bits of kind and
twenty-eight of id suits one consumer; a consumer with four domains pays for
four it cannot use, and one with sixteen cannot have them. That is a consumer's
choice wearing a substrate constant.

A shape names four widths: how many bits tag the domain, how many name the
minter, how many count within a minter, and the one flag bit. The last three
divide what the `Standard` table calls `id`. It also supplies the origin type,
where a minter's counter starts and stops, and how a counter projects into the
id field, so the one low-level numeric edge of a producer sits with the shape
that decides how wide the field is.

**A shape brings its own layout**, not merely its own division of a shared one.
That is what lets the kind width vary: a shape declaring a wider tag declares a
bitfield with a wider tag, and the positions stay literals inside the impl that
writes them.

A width the trait declares is a width something **constrains**, and the
constraint has to be derived rather than written beside the thing it checks.

A layout names the **types** its accessors carry: `Kind` and `Id`, each a `Bits`
at that field's real width. The width is read off those types by `FieldWidth`,
whose blanket impl over `Bits` yields the width parameter, and a law ties each
shape's declared width to it. No layout can supply a different answer, because
the impl is blanket and specialisation is forbidden here.

**That is half the guarantee, and stating it as the whole was the last mistake
this class produced.** The type pins what the accessors are compiled against. It
does not pin the field behind them, because an accessor may convert: a layout
declaring a nine-bit `Kind` over a genuine five-bit field, widening on the way
out and truncating on the way in, satisfies every signature and passes every law
that reads a declared width against a declared type.

What pins the field is the **round trip**. Writing a field's maximum and reading
it back unchanged fails the moment the field behind the accessor is narrower
than the type in front of it, and the whole-handle law fails alongside it,
because the bits the shrunken field vacates then belong to nothing.

So the route runs declaration, to type, to round trip, to bits that land, and
each link carries part of it. Neither end alone is enough, and the previous four
attempts each named one link and called the question closed.

**Tying a declaration to another declaration constrains neither**, which is the
general form and what this crate learned five times over. A law over two
hand-written numbers passes when both are wrong together. A law over a mask
constant passes when the mask is wrong, because no accessor reads that constant.
A law over an associated type passes when the accessor converts. Each looked
like a derivation and was a second statement of one unchecked fact.

The test a width must pass is whether the route from its declaration reaches a
value the code **uses**, and the cheapest way to find out is to write the layout
that lies and watch what fails. Nothing here was established by reading.

**A known limit, stated rather than left in a signature.** `origin_base`,
`origin_ceiling` and `id_from_counter` are typed `Uint<28, Hot>` in the trait.
Twenty-eight is `Standard`'s id width, sitting at the one place a shape cannot
override, which is the same substance as the constant this design lifted, one
level down and not yet lifted. The two laws above contain it: a shape whose
origins do not fit its own `ID_BITS` fails them. Making the type follow the shape
is open.

`SymShape` is a const trait, so a handle builds in a const context under any
shape. A domain declares which shape it belongs to, which is what stops a domain
of one shape handing its tag to a producer of another.

## What the tests must cover

Every effect, boundary and edge case this document describes carries a unit
test. A documented claim nothing tests is the same defect as a declared width
nothing constrains: prose asserting a property, with no value that would make it
fail. The widths reached their fifth fix because the laws around them were read
for their names rather than for what they could catch.

Each is asserted for **every** shape, never the default alone, since choosing
which instantiations to check is choosing what not to find out.

Each accessor round-trips at its field's real width, at zero, at the field
maximum, and at a value with alternating bits. The maximum is the case that
finds a field one bit too narrow, and the alternating value is the one that
finds a shift in the wrong direction.

Writing one field disturbs no other. This is the bitfield failure every law here
was previously blind to, because a law that reads one field at a time cannot see
an overlap between two.

A zeroed layout reads zero in every field, and the raw bits agree with the
fields. Handle equality is raw-bit equality, which this document calls the
crate's central promise and which nothing asserted.

A minter refuses at its own ceiling and keeps refusing. The first mint of the
last id must succeed and the next must not, at both ends of the span, since a
boundary tested from one side is half tested.

Every id belongs to exactly one origin, checked at the first and last id of each
origin's span rather than at an arbitrary interior point.

**Two origins never produce one handle**, minted rather than reasoned about, for
every origin of every shape. This is the crate's own promise and it went unstated
as a law for nine rounds. Six laws related declarations to each other and to
layout types, and a shape could still mint colliding handles, because none of
them minted anything.

**Every origin's base and ceiling fit that shape's `ID_BITS`.** Without it a
shape may lay origins out for a wider id space than it has, and the overflowing
base wraps to another origin's. That is the collision above with a specific
cause, and the two laws are separate because one names the property and the other
names the mechanism.

The general form, met six times in this crate: **a law over declarations is not a
law over behaviour.** A width law cannot see a collision; only minting can. Every
time this defect moved, the fix related one declaration to another, and the sixth
time it moved past declarations entirely and into an origin's arithmetic.

The widths are related by arithmetic, since they must total 32. **That
arithmetic lives in each impl rather than in a bound.** Writing the derivation
inline would put an expression in type position, which this stack's toolchain
policy forbids; carrying it as an associated type on a trait puts it where
expressions are legal and leaves every bound naming a contract.

`Standard` is the default and is byte-identical to the layout above: kind 3,
one minter, counter 28. **The string domain's fixed `0b000` tag and every baked
string handle survive it unchanged**, because nothing about the default moves.

`SixteenMinters` is the second shape: same kind width, four bits naming which of
sixteen minters produced a handle, twenty-four counting within it. It varies how
the id divides.

`WideKind` is the third and varies the tag itself: **five bits of kind**, thirty
two domains, twenty-six of id. It exists because a trait whose every
implementation shares one layout has not demonstrated the thing it was lifted
for, and until one shape's tag is a different width the kind width is a
parameter over a single value.

## `SymKind`, the domain tag

```rust
#[repr(transparent)]
#[derive(Copy, Clone, Eq, PartialEq, Debug)]
pub struct SymKind<S: SymShape = Standard>(<S::Layout as SymLayoutOps>::Kind);
```

No `Hash`, and that is a consequence rather than an omission. The inner type is
`SymLayoutOps::Kind`, which is bounded `Copy + Eq + Debug`, so deriving `Hash`
here would mean requiring it of every layout's tag type to serve one consumer
that has not asked. `Sym` itself derives `Hash`, and a handle is what goes in a
map.

The tag naming which domain a handle belongs to, at whatever width its shape
gives it. `SymKind::new` builds one from a raw value. Under `Standard` that is
three bits and eight domains; the flag bit is a per-domain extra orthogonal to
the tag under every shape.

The kind-assignment table below is per-shape. It is `Standard`'s.

The kind-assignment table is a documented convention owned here so two
domains cannot claim one tag. The current assignments:

| Kind | Domain |
|---|---|
| `0b000` | string domain. Its const and runtime origins are the flag bit (const 0, runtime 1), so both are one domain, not two tags. The tag is fixed at `0b000` because it equals the old string layout's reserved-field value and the flag equals the old origin bit, which keeps every baked string handle byte-identical. |
| `0b001` | a minted-binder domain used by a downstream compiler consumer, defined in that consumer's crate, recorded here so nothing else claims the tag. |
| `0b010` to `0b111` | free for future domains. |

## `Domain`, `InterningDomain`, `GenerativeDomain`

```rust
pub trait Domain {
    type Shape: SymShape;

    const KIND: SymKind<Self::Shape>;
}

pub trait InterningDomain: Domain {
    type Value: ?Sized;
}

pub trait GenerativeDomain: Domain {}
```

A domain is a marker type carrying its `kind` as an associated constant.
It refines into one of two modes. An `InterningDomain` names the value it
deduplicates. A `GenerativeDomain` marks the mint-only mode and names no
value. The refinement is what the producers bind against, so the mode is
a type-level fact and the wrong producer for a domain is a type error.

## `Interner`, the dedup-and-resolve producer

```rust
pub trait Interner<D: InterningDomain> {
    fn intern(&self, value: &D::Value) -> Sym<D::Shape>;
    fn resolve(&self, sym: Sym<D::Shape>) -> Maybe<&D::Value>;
}
```

The trait for a producer that folds a value to a stable handle and
resolves it back. `intern` returns a `Sym` tagged `D::KIND`; equal values
give equal handles (a within-domain flag may still distinguish origins,
as strings do). `resolve` returns the value for a handle of this domain,
or `Maybe::Isnt` for a handle the producer does not know. The string
interner in `hilavitkutin-str` is the first implementation: `StringInterner`
implements `Interner` for the single string domain, with the const linker
section plus a host arena as its backing and the flag bit selecting which
side resolves a handle.

## `Generator`, the mint-only producer

```rust
pub struct Generator<D: GenerativeDomain> {
    // a counter and a ceiling within one origin's range, plus a domain marker.
    // The shape comes from the domain rather than a second parameter.
}
```

The concrete producer for a generative domain. It holds a monotonic counter and
mints a fresh handle on demand:

```rust
// mint returns a fresh Sym<D::Shape> tagged D::KIND, or Isnt at this
// origin's ceiling.
pub fn mint(&mut self) -> Maybe<Sym>;
```

At the counter ceiling it returns `Maybe::Isnt` rather than wrapping and
reissuing a live id, so freshness is never silently violated. No allocation, no
arena.

### What freshness means here, exactly

**A minted `Sym` is distinct from every prior mint of the same generator at the
same origin. It is not globally unique, and the difference has bitten.**

Two generators for one domain, each starting its counter at zero, emit
bit-identical handles. `Sym` compares by integer equality over the whole
layout, which is this crate's central promise, so those handles do not merely
fail to match: they compare **equal**. A consumer designing on top of this crate
measured 132 of 144 peer scenarios false-identifying, including 24 in which the
two peers shared no value at all.

Incomparable would have been safe, because a wrong answer a consumer can detect
is a wrong answer it can handle. Falsely equal is silent, and nothing looks
broken.

### Origins make disjointness constructible

So a generator is built **for an origin**, and origins divide the counter space
by construction rather than by convention. Each origin starts at its own base and
stops at its own ceiling, so two minters at two origins cannot produce the same
id however far either counts, and a minter that exhausts its range refuses rather
than walking into its neighbour's.

Under `Standard` there is one origin and zero bits naming it, so the counter
takes the full 28 bits and `Generator::single` needs no argument. Nothing changes
for a consumer with one minter, and there is no second origin for it to name.

A consumer with several independent minters declares a domain on a shape with
origin bits, and then `Generator::at` is the only way in: a generator cannot be
constructed without saying which minter it is. Two peers colliding would require
both to claim the same origin, which is a declared conflict rather than a silent
default.

This is the same principle the exhaustion contract already states. The crate
refuses to reissue a live id; two peers reissuing each other's ids is that
fault arriving from the one direction a single counter cannot see.

## Why a monomorphic handle plus typed producers

The handle is monomorphic (`Sym`, not a phantom `Sym<D>` parameterised by
domain). A phantom-typed handle would type-check the intern and mint
edges, but every field that can hold either domain (a compiler binder
that is a source name or a minted name) must erase it to a common type,
so the phantom buys type-checking only at a few edges the runtime `kind`
and the typed producers already cover, at the cost of an erasure
boundary. The handle stays monomorphic for storage; `Interner` and
`Generator` carry the domain type for the safety that matters.

The shape parameter is a different thing and does not reintroduce that
argument. A shape is a storage format rather than a domain, and a store holds
one format, so parameterising by shape creates no erasure boundary: the
heterogeneous case the phantom domain would have forced does not arise, because
two shapes were never comparable in the first place.

## Design rule

No alloc. No std. No ecosystem dependency: this crate sits directly on
`arvo`, `arvo-bits`, and `notko`, so any consumer can build on it without
pulling in the string machinery. String interning is one domain, supplied
by `hilavitkutin-str`; it is not privileged here.
